      "id": "SFPL",
      "code_hash": "ee3813fdc4aa85eaefeab27840f398f2",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h2 id=\"3-county-data-attached-to-hospitals-the-ecological-fallacy\">3. County data attached to hospitals: the ecological fallacy</h2>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">If your layer joins a <strong>county</strong> measure (CDC PLACES, ACS) to <strong>hospitals</strong>, every hospital in a county\ngets the <em>same</em> county value. In the course crosswalk, <strong>88 hospitals</strong> sit in\nLos Angeles County, CA (FIPS 06037), and 897 counties have more than one hospital.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">A county value describes <strong>people who live in the county</strong> \u2014 it is a fact about a place, not about any\nhospital's patients. So \"hospitals in high-poverty counties have higher readmissions\" is a statement\nabout counties. It does <strong>not</strong> show that poorer <em>patients</em> are readmitted more, and it can't tell you\nanything about one patient. Saying otherwise is the ecological fallacy. Patients also cross county lines.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\"><strong>Coverage gap to put on your data card:</strong> 325 hospitals that have a county FIPS get <strong>no</strong>\nCDC PLACES 2025 value at all, because their county code is not in the PLACES file (by state: PA 186, KY 103, CT 36).\nFind out why before you explain it \u2014 don't guess.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
